Build the real fbdevhw, not the stub, and let it open the framebuffer.

Three changes, all needed before a framebuffer can be driven here.

1. configure decides between hw/xfree86/fbdevhw/fbdevhw.c and fbdevhwstub.c on
   whether <linux/fb.h> exists.  The interface is the same here, the header is
   called <sys/fb.h>, so the test accepts either.

   That test is worth more than it looks: the stub exports no
   fbdevhwModuleData, so the server loads the module, finds no module data, and
   gives up with

       (EE) Module fbdevhw does not have a fbdevhwModuleData data object
       (EE) No devices detected

   Nothing in that names a missing header.

2. fbdevhw.c takes the system header in place of the tree's own fbpriv.h copy.
   The structures are an ioctl ABI, and keeping two independent descriptions of
   one ABI is how they come to disagree.

3. fbdev_open() asks sysfs whether the framebuffer belongs to a PCI device, so
   a PCI-aware driver can claim it instead.  Neither half applies here: there
   is no sysfs to ask, and no PCI bus reachable from userspace.  It cannot be
   left to fail either -- a readlink() returning -1 satisfies the condition, so
   the device is closed and rejected, and the server again reports "No devices
   detected" without mentioning either sysfs or the framebuffer it had just
   opened successfully.

--- a/configure.ac
+++ b/configure.ac
@@ -209,7 +209,18 @@
 AM_CONDITIONAL(AGP, [test "x$AGP" = xyes])
 
 dnl fbdev header
-AC_CHECK_HEADERS([linux/fb.h], FBDEV=yes)
+dnl
+dnl Two spellings, because the header's name is not the same everywhere while
+dnl the interface behind it is.  hw/xfree86/fbdevhw/Makefile.am builds the real
+dnl fbdevhw.c only when this is set and fbdevhwstub.c otherwise -- and the stub
+dnl exports no fbdevhwModuleData at all, so the server loads it, finds no module
+dnl data, and reports
+dnl
+dnl     (EE) Module fbdevhw does not have a fbdevhwModuleData data object
+dnl     (EE) No devices detected
+dnl
+dnl which says nothing about a missing header.
+AC_CHECK_HEADERS([linux/fb.h sys/fb.h], FBDEV=yes)
 AM_CONDITIONAL(FBDEVHW, [test "x$FBDEV" = xyes])
 
 dnl FreeBSD kldload support (sys/linker.h)
--- a/hw/xfree86/fbdevhw/fbdevhw.c
+++ b/hw/xfree86/fbdevhw/fbdevhw.c
@@ -15,7 +15,15 @@
 #include "xf86cmap.h"
 
 #include "fbdevhw.h"
+/* fbpriv.h is this tree's own copy of the framebuffer definitions, kept for
+ * platforms whose headers do not provide them.  Where the system has an
+ * authoritative header it must be preferred: the structures are an ioctl ABI,
+ * and having two independent descriptions of it is how they drift apart. */
+#ifdef __LIKEOS__
+#include <sys/fb.h>
+#else
 #include "fbpriv.h"
+#endif
 #include "globals.h"
 #include <X11/extensions/dpmsconst.h>
 
@@ -330,6 +338,7 @@
     }
 
     /* only touch non-PCI devices on this path */
+#ifndef __LIKEOS__
     {
         char buf[PATH_MAX] = {0};
         char *sysfs_path = NULL;
@@ -344,6 +353,18 @@
         }
         free(sysfs_path);
     }
+#else
+    /* The check above asks sysfs whether this framebuffer belongs to a PCI
+     * device, so that a PCI-aware driver can claim it instead.  Neither half
+     * of that applies here: there is no sysfs to ask, and no PCI bus reachable
+     * from userspace for the answer to be about.
+     *
+     * It cannot be left to fail, either.  A readlink() returning -1 satisfies
+     * the condition, so the framebuffer that was just opened successfully is
+     * CLOSED and rejected -- and the server reports "No devices detected",
+     * naming neither sysfs nor the device.
+     */
+#endif
 
